  
Vuln-bank penetration testing report   

Target application: vuln-bank  
Testing window: 8 December

**Executive summary** 
In this lab, I gained hands-on experience exploiting API vulnerabilities on a Vulnerable banking application. I was able to find vulnerabilities such as broken object-level authorization (BOLA), broken object property-level authorization (BOPLA), and broken authentication. 

**Methodology:**  
This testing used a gray box technique

**Tools used**   
Burp Suite (intercepting and testing)  
Docker (hosting the application)

**Key findings**  
Broken object property authentication: when adding a user to the account, I noticed there was mass assignment. Many sensitive fields were disclosed even when creating a simple user. I added a user to the system and added more fields to the user (is\_admin and Balance), and the user was created successfully![][image1]  
![][image2]  
![][image3]

Broken authentication: for broken authentication,

1.  I was able to reuse my old token to log back into the user's account

![][image4]

2. I was able to tamper with some fields in the JWT token and still use it successfully on the app  
   ![][image5]  
   ![][image6]  
   ![][image7]

Broken object-level authorization:  
To test for BOLA, I input the account numbers of various users in the /transactions and /check\_balance endpoints. With this, I was able to retrieve sensitive information relating to their account balance and Transaction history  
